---
import Base from '../../layouts/Base.astro';
import { fxNote, type Currency } from '../../lib/currency';
import { dataFor } from '../../lib/data';
import { currencyPaths } from '../../lib/routes';
import {
  cellViews,
  diagonalGapNote as gapNote,
  holdingTiles,
  HEAT_STEPS,
  partialYears,
} from '../../lib/holding-shared';

export const getStaticPaths = currencyPaths;

const { currency } = Astro.props as { currency: Currency };
const dataset = dataFor(currency).holdingPeriods;

const tiles = holdingTiles(dataset);
const cells = cellViews(dataset);
const { summary } = dataset;
const years = dataset.years.map((y) => y.year);
const firstYear = years[0];
const lastYear = years.at(-1);
const wholeYears = dataset.years.filter((y) => y.whole);
const partial = partialYears(dataset);
const partialList = partial.join(' or ');
const partialDates = dataset.years
  .filter((y) => !y.whole)
  .map((y) => `${y.year} runs ${y.basisDate} to ${y.closeDate}`)
  .join('; ');
const losing = summary.count - summary.positive;
const diagonalGapNote = gapNote(dataset);
---

<Base
  currency={currency}
  title="blockplot · holding periods"
  description="Every buy year against every sell year: what each hold returned, annualised, and how long you had to hold to never be down."
>
  <h1 class="visually-hidden">Holding periods</h1>

  <dl class="stat-grid">
    {
      tiles.map((tile) => (
        <div class="stat">
          <dt>{tile.label}</dt>
          <dd class={`num ${tile.tone}`}>{tile.value}</dd>
          <dd class="sub num">{tile.sub}</dd>
        </div>
      ))
    }
  </dl>

  <section class="chart-section">
    <h2>Bought at the start of, sold at the end of</h2>
    <div class="table-scroll">
      <table class="risk-table heatmap holding-matrix">
        <caption class="visually-hidden">
          Annualised return for every hold from a buy year to a sell year. Rows are the year
          bought, columns the year sold; a cell's description gives the total return and the
          number of years held.
        </caption>
        <thead>
          <tr>
            <th scope="col">Buy \ sell</th>
            {
              years.map((year) => (
                <th scope="col" class="num">
                  {String(year).slice(2)}
                  {partial.includes(year) && <span class="mark">*</span>}
                </th>
              ))
            }
          </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          {
            years.map((buyYear) => (
              <tr>
                <th scope="row" class="num">
                  {buyYear}
                  {partial.includes(buyYear) && <span class="mark">*</span>}
                </th>
                {years.map((sellYear) => {
                  const cell = cells.get(`${buyYear}-${sellYear}`);
                  // An empty cell, not a hidden one. `aria-hidden` on the 136
                  // blanks removed them from the accessibility tree, which leaves
                  // rows with different column counts in the table model screen
                  // readers use to track position — so the fix for "don't announce
                  // a triangle as a hole" broke navigating the grid at all. An
                  // empty `<td>` announces as "blank", which is the convention and
                  // what the monthly heatmap already does for missing months.
                  return cell === undefined ? (
                    <td class="num blank" />
                  ) : (
                    // The rate is the cell's accessible name, so the grid can be
                    // skimmed; the sentence is its description, reached on demand.
                    // Both were present before — the sentence in a visually-hidden
                    // span *and* in `title` — so a reader with object descriptions
                    // on heard the same ninety characters twice, 153 times.
                    <td class={`num ${cell.heat}`} title={cell.title}>{cell.label}</td>
                  );
                })}
              </tr>
            ))
          }
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>

    <p class="method-note">
      Each cell is one hold: bought at the start of the row's year, sold at the end of the
      column's. The figure is the <strong>annualised</strong> rate, because that is the only one
      comparable across the grid — a 300% total is extraordinary over one year and ordinary over
      ten — and each cell's description carries the total return and the number of years held. The
      lower-left half is empty because selling before you bought is not a hold.
    </p>
    <p class="method-note">
      A year is priced from the last close of the previous December to the last close of its own,
      which is not the obvious choice and is the one that reconciles: the <em>total return</em> of
      each hold on the diagonal is exactly the yearly figure the{' '}
      <a href={`${import.meta.env.BASE_URL}${currency === 'usd' ? '' : 'gbp/'}`}>overview</a>
      {' '}heatmap publishes for the same year. Anchoring on the first close of the buy year
      instead would put the two pages a few days out from each other, with nothing saying which to
      believe. What the diagonal <em>displays</em> is the annualised rate, and for a one-year hold
      those two differ slightly: a calendar year is 365 days and the annualisation divides by
      365.2425, so a 365-day hold is priced as marginally less than a year and its rate comes out
      marginally above its total — {diagonalGapNote}. The reconciliation is on the total, which
      every cell's description states.
      {partial.length > 0 && (
        <>
          {' '}The history is truncated at {partial.length === 1 ? 'one end' : 'both ends'}, and the
          grid marks {partial.length === 1 ? 'it' : 'them'} with an asterisk: {partialDates}. A hold
          bought or sold in {partialList} is anchored on those dates rather than on a January or a
          December, and each cell's description names the date rather than saying "end of" a year
          that has not ended. {partial.length === 1 ? 'Its' : 'Their'} own single-year hold
          {partial.length === 1 ? '' : 's'} run under {dataset.minAnnualiseDays} days and carry no
          annualised rate at all — below a year that figure is an extrapolation rather than a
          measurement.
        </>
      )}
    </p>
    <p class="method-note">
      Colour is fixed to the rate, not to this dataset's range, so the same shade means the same
      magnitude on every refresh: bands break at {HEAT_STEPS.join('%, ')}% a year either side of
      zero. The negative side uses the same numbers even though nothing here reaches −{HEAT_STEPS[2]}%,
      because an asymmetric scale would make a −60% and a +60% look like different magnitudes.
    </p>
    <p class="method-note">
      A hold's length is counted in calendar years: bought at the start of {firstYear + 1} and sold
      at the end of {firstYear + 2} is two years, because the anchors are a December apart.
      {partial.length > 0 && (
        <>
          {' '}The asterisked {partial.length === 1 ? 'year is an' : 'years are the'} exception — a
          hold touching one is shorter than its label by however much of that year is missing.
        </>
      )}
      {' '}Each cell states its own duration in days or years rather than leaving you to infer it
      from the two headings.
    </p>
    <p class="method-note">
      What this cannot tell you: it describes {wholeYears.length} whole years of one asset that
      existed throughout them, which is the definition of a survivorship-shaped sample. "Every
      {' '}{summary.safeYears}-year hold ended up" is a fact about {firstYear}–{lastYear} and not a
      property of the asset — {losing} of {summary.count} holds here did end down, and the longest
      losing one ran {summary.longestLosing?.days} days. Returns are price returns: no fees, no
      taxes, no dividends, and a single purchase rather than the drip the{' '}
      <a href={`${import.meta.env.BASE_URL}${currency === 'usd' ? '' : 'gbp/'}dca/`}>DCA page</a> models.{fxNote(currency)}
    </p>
  </section>
</Base>
